  "notes": "Basaa (Ɓasaá), Bantu A.43a (Guthrie), spoken by c. 300,000 people in the South, Centre and Littoral regions of Cameroon (Hyman, Basaa chapter; Hartell 1993, Alphabets of Africa). Latin orthography with African-reference-alphabet letters ɓ ɛ ŋ ɔ and ten multigraphs gw hy mb nj ny nd ŋg ŋgw ŋw kw. Vowel length is written by doubling the letter and is phonemic (Hyman). Four tones: high and low are register tones, rising and falling are contours; low is orthographically unmarked, the others take diacritics over the vowel. ⟨mb nd nj ŋg ŋgw⟩ are prenasalised stops/affricates, single segments in the phonology, not consonant clusters. Root-medial, post-pausal /p t k/ lenite to voiced stops/fricatives ([b~β] [d~ð] [ɡ~ɣ]); recorded as an allophone since this is a general lenition process rather than a distinct spelling.",
